  "knowledgePattern": "Recognise a squared quadratic and count distinct roots",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "algebra"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The first three terms form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, giving the two equations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The first gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. The second gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>These are three distinct real numbers. The repeated root at 1 counts once. Answer D.</p>",
